WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.930
- This is my favorite
thing I actually learned.

00:00:01.930 --> 00:00:04.370
I feel like I was talking to
Sophia when I learned that

00:00:04.370 --> 00:00:06.510
you're really into "Dungeons and Dragons."

00:00:06.510 --> 00:00:08.120
And you have like a club.

00:00:08.120 --> 00:00:08.953
- Yeah, sure.

00:00:08.953 --> 00:00:11.530
You know, I played as a
kid and then as an adult

00:00:11.530 --> 00:00:14.670
I actually, I like work with the company

00:00:14.670 --> 00:00:16.420
which is the job I wanted as a kid.

00:00:16.420 --> 00:00:19.410
So I get to write stories
that have been published

00:00:19.410 --> 00:00:21.270
you know, in their adventure modules.

00:00:21.270 --> 00:00:23.270
I created a bunch of characters that

00:00:23.270 --> 00:00:26.210
are sold in packages like action figures.

00:00:26.210 --> 00:00:27.043
- [Kelly] Yeah.
- [Joe] And yeah,

00:00:27.043 --> 00:00:27.940
this is my game.

00:00:27.940 --> 00:00:30.000
That's my brother with the big beard.

00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:32.300
There's me, I built this whole ice castle

00:00:32.300 --> 00:00:33.600
on the dining room table.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:34.680
That's Tom Morello,

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:36.670
the guitarist from Rage
Against the Machine.

00:00:36.670 --> 00:00:39.000
There's, Vince Vaughn.

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:40.070
- [Kelly] This is crazy.
- [Joe] Dan Weiss

00:00:40.070 --> 00:00:41.860
co-creator of "Game of Thrones."

00:00:41.860 --> 00:00:42.830
Director Kyle Newman.

00:00:42.830 --> 00:00:45.427
That's Ryan Vernier who helped to create

00:00:45.427 --> 00:00:46.360
"League of Legends."

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:48.840
That's my buddy George in the front,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:51.403
who's was a great actor.
- Look how excited you are.

00:00:51.403 --> 00:00:53.740
- Yeah, I mean, we're just having a blast.

00:00:53.740 --> 00:00:55.590
And, so this is my game that I run

00:00:55.590 --> 00:00:57.270
every Friday night that
I'm not working in there

00:00:57.270 --> 00:00:58.130
or that I'm in town.

00:00:58.130 --> 00:01:00.372
And this is the whole group was a bunch of

00:01:00.372 --> 00:01:03.100
like visionaries in,

00:01:03.100 --> 00:01:06.130
you know, A list people in
the active entertainment world

00:01:06.130 --> 00:01:09.740
who grew up and developed
those skills playing this game.

00:01:09.740 --> 00:01:11.530
- You've actually used
"Dungeons and Dragons"

00:01:11.530 --> 00:01:12.870
as a way to help kids, right?

00:01:12.870 --> 00:01:13.703
Sick kids.

00:01:13.703 --> 00:01:15.550
- Yeah, I'm on the board of trustees

00:01:15.550 --> 00:01:16.860
at Pittsburgh Children's Hospital

00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.920
and have been for a long time.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:19.753
And,

00:01:21.320 --> 00:01:23.100
I had this idea, you know,

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.060
what, I visit the hospital
I visited the kids and

00:01:26.060 --> 00:01:28.740
there were a lot of these kids who

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:32.300
are there battling really
serious illnesses and,

00:01:32.300 --> 00:01:34.330
sometimes they're in the
hospital for months at a time

00:01:34.330 --> 00:01:36.230
and their parents are working.

00:01:36.230 --> 00:01:37.730
So they don't have anybody there

00:01:37.730 --> 00:01:39.730
from their immediate
family staying with them,

00:01:39.730 --> 00:01:40.740
except for the other kids.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.440
And, sometimes kids are hooked up to an IV

00:01:43.440 --> 00:01:46.110
for hours at a time getting
an infusion or something.

00:01:46.110 --> 00:01:48.930
And, I thought,

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:52.610
one of the beauty beautiful
things about "D and D" is that

00:01:52.610 --> 00:01:55.830
you go into this other
world and you're not you.

00:01:55.830 --> 00:01:57.360
And it's a super immersive.

00:01:57.360 --> 00:01:58.847
- That's what I like
fantasy stuff like that.

00:01:58.847 --> 00:02:01.340
- And I thought maybe that
would work for the kids.

00:02:01.340 --> 00:02:05.040
That at least for a few hours
they could not have to be them

00:02:05.040 --> 00:02:06.990
and deal with what they were dealing with

00:02:06.990 --> 00:02:09.520
and escape and play a character.

00:02:09.520 --> 00:02:10.880
And it works.

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:11.930
And so I had "Dungeons and Dragons"

00:02:11.930 --> 00:02:14.040
send crates full of boxes and dice.

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.050
And I, my brother and
I went to the hospital

00:02:16.050 --> 00:02:17.730
and taught the kids how to play.

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:21.280
And I had dungeon master like
an adventure with goblins and

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:22.720
you know, things like that for them.

00:02:22.720 --> 00:02:25.040
And, they just, they loved it.

00:02:25.040 --> 00:02:28.530
And, I hope it's carried on.

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:30.110
But we tried to plant that seed

00:02:30.110 --> 00:02:32.660
that this was a way for
you to kinda get out of

00:02:32.660 --> 00:02:33.960
the everyday.
- Absolutely.

00:02:33.960 --> 00:02:36.250
And that escapism is so
important for everyone.

00:02:36.250 --> 00:02:37.750
And especially if you're stuck

00:02:37.750 --> 00:02:40.060
and I think this year everybody
can relate to that more than

00:02:40.060 --> 00:02:41.770
anybody, we're all kind of stuck inside

00:02:41.770 --> 00:02:43.270
and not doing our normal,

00:02:43.270 --> 00:02:44.220
you know, routines.

